1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,671
If you operate a diode laser engraver like Sculpfun, Atomstack, xTool, Ortur, or Creality and use LightBurn, you face two major headaches. First, running long USB cables across your workshop causes signal degradation, electromagnetic noise, and sudden disconnects that ruin expensive workpieces. Second, VirtualHere's free tier only allows a single device, forcing you to pay forty-nine dollars per machine just to connect your laser and camera at the same time. SnakeUSBIP solves this completely by connecting both simultaneously with zero licensing fees.

2
00:00:29,671 --> 00:00:54,977
The physical setup in your workshop is tidy and reliable. Place a budget-friendly single-board computer, like a Raspberry Pi, Orange Pi, or Linux mini-PC, right next to your laser enclosure. Into USB port one, plug your laser controller, typically running a CH340 or CP2102 chip. Into USB port two, plug your LightBurn overhead alignment camera. From this point onward, no cables snake across the floor: all communications travel securely over Wi-Fi or Ethernet.

3
00:00:54,977 --> 00:01:18,147
Configuring the server on your Raspberry Pi takes less than a minute. Connect over SSH and run our official one-line command. The installer downloads the USB/IP kernel packages, activates the system daemon, and deploys intelligent udev rules. As a result, the moment you power on your laser and camera, both devices are instantly shared over your network automatically, with zero manual binding commands required.

4
00:01:18,147 --> 00:01:40,481
On your design computer running LightBurn, download the official SnakeUSBIP installer. It packages virtual bus drivers certified directly by Microsoft through official WHQL attestation. This guarantees seamless compatibility on Windows 10 and Windows 11, working out of the box with Core Isolation and Memory Integrity enabled, supporting both 64-bit Intel/AMD processors and modern ARM64 laptops.

5
00:01:40,481 --> 00:02:05,694
When you launch SnakeUSBIP, its broadcast engine discovers your Raspberry Pi in seconds, showing live ping latency in milliseconds. You will see your laser's serial controller and your webcam listed. Click Connect on both. Windows creates a virtual COM port for the laser and a standard UVC video device for the camera. Check the Auto-connect box on both peripherals so they automatically re-attach whenever the laser is powered on or rebooted.

6
00:02:05,694 --> 00:02:27,981
From the bottom status bar, you can switch network engine modes on the fly. For your laser controller, Low-Latency WSK mode utilizes direct kernel callbacks to eliminate packet jitter during G-code streaming, making manual jogging and laser framing in LightBurn respond instantly. For your overhead camera, Zero-Copy mode provides maximum network throughput for high-resolution, stutter-free video feeds.

7
00:02:27,981 --> 00:02:53,798
Now open LightBurn. In the Devices window, choose GRBL via Serial and select the virtual COM port generated by SnakeUSBIP. The console will instantly reply, confirming communication with your laser. Next, open the Camera Control window, choose the remote USB camera from the dropdown list, and complete the lens calibration and visual alignment. You can now place wood or acrylic on your honeycomb bed, align artwork with millimeter precision, and start engraving right from your desk.

8
00:02:53,798 --> 00:03:21,240
If your laser engraver sits in a detached garage, backyard shed, or remote workshop, you never need to mess with router firewall rules or expose vulnerable ports to the internet. SnakeUSBIP features native compatibility with encrypted mesh VPNs like Tailscale, ZeroTier, and WireGuard. Simply click Add Server, enter your Raspberry Pi's Tailscale IP address, and you can control and visually monitor your laser from the comfort of your living room or remote office with complete security.

9
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:46,593
In conclusion: simultaneous and unlimited connection of your diode laser and camera in LightBurn, zero mid-burn disconnects from long cables, fault-tolerant auto-reconnect, low latency for pinpoint framing, and secure VPN routing. All of this without paying forty-nine dollar per-device licenses and with officially certified drivers. Download SnakeUSBIP today from our official GitHub repository, support the project with a star, and enjoy complete freedom in your laser workshop.

